Aerials — screensaver footage
=============================

Drop a video file in this directory and the screensaver will dissolve from
the warped desktop into it, looping for as long as the saver is up.

    /usr/share/starling/aerials/          system-wide (this directory)
    ~/.local/share/starling/aerials/      per user
    $STARLING_AERIAL                      a file or directory, wins over both

The first clip by filename is used. Recognised extensions: mp4, mov, m4v,
webm, mkv.

Starling ships no footage of its own. The screensaver is complete without
it — with no clip installed it shows a slow liquid warp over the live
desktop, which needs no assets — and the aerial clips worth watching run to
tens of megabytes, which is a poor thing to put on every install.

Any resolution and aspect ratio works. Letterbox bars baked into the file
are detected and cropped, and the result is cover-cropped to fill the panel,
so a clip that doesn't match your display's shape is filled rather than
pillarboxed.

Decoding is done by ffmpeg (the .deb Recommends it; without it the saver
stays on the warp). Frames are decoded at the size they are shown, which on
a 4K panel is a real amount of work — set STARLING_AERIAL_RES=1920x1080 (or
lower) if playback stutters on your machine.
